  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Walker Buehler did his job. The offense didn't answer.",
    "what_this_means": "They trailed from the second inning onward and never recovered. Buehler held the Mets to two runs in six innings. Clean, controlled, exactly what you ask from a starter in a close game. Kyle Hart bridged two scoreless frames. The pitching was sound. But seven hits and one run in a bases-loaded situation early told the story: they had the moment and let it pass. That's the game. Runners on in the first, two outs, no conversion. The rest of the night played out in the shadow of that inning. Inconsistency is the thread. And today it was the specific kind that kills you: good enough pitching asking the offense to deliver, and the offense answering with silence.",
    "what_to_watch": "Zac Thornton has a 2.78 ERA and the Mets are riding him tonight. Robbie Ray has found his form in his last four starts, sitting at 3.00. He'll need every bit of that control facing a lineup that just beat the Padres' best pitching effort of the week.",
